Entrepreneurship helps develop the economy by raising productivity, creating new employment opportunities, and creating and revitalizing markets through new products and improved product development
Entrepreneurship & the Economy 
MACROECONOMIC
•	Capital Formation  Employment growth  Technological progress  Knowledge-based development 
MICROECONOMIC
•	Establishment of new economic entities  Creation of new jobs  Production of new goods and services  Creation of niche markets
According to R. Cantillon, the fundamental role of the entrepreneur is to make decisions in an uncertain economic context. An entrepreneur is described as a risk-taker who bears uncertainty.
J. A. Schumpeter underlines that the entrepreneur is the economic individual who realizes new “productive combinations”.
Typical opportunities for entrepreneurs:
 1) New good or of a new quality of good. 
2) New method of production
3) New market 
4) New source of supply of raw material or half-manufactured goods 
5) New organization of any industry
Neoclassical Economics 
•	Marshal presented the entrepreneur as an individual who is both a risktaker and an administrator.  Responsible for ensuring production function in a company, identifying opportunities, reducing costs and increasing profits. 
•	Classified entrepreneurs as: Active entrepreneurs (those who find new ways), and Passive entrepreneurs (those who tend to follow the existing road) 
Functions that are involved in the entrepreneurial activity: 
•	Collection of information regarding economic situations. 
•	Economic calculations in order to make the production process efficient. 
•	Assigning goods to a particular production process. 
•	Supervising the execution of the production plan and making sure that it is carried out in an economical way.
Entreprenuership vs management
•	Entrepreneurship is a dynamic process of constantly changing the economic structure and its effectiveness. An entrepreneur implies a person who is ready to take his/her own risk and introduce new ideas and economic projects and activities. 
•	A manager is responsible for taking actions that will allow the maximum contribution of individuals to the tasks of the whole group, and as such refers to small and large organizations, for-profit and non-profit enterprises, to the production and provision of services.
Entrepreneurial management
•	Entrepreneurial management implies the management of a company and facility based on constant innovation and focused achievement of business goals. 
•	The unification of entrepreneurial and managerial functions within a business organization in order to achieve the growth and development of the company.
Tourism entreprenuership
•	From the point of view of entrepreneurship in tourism the main goal is to attract and develop those jobs that can compete, grow and create jobs on the market. 
•	From the point of view of (micro) economic entities in tourism, it is necessary first of all to find a way that will ensure their long-term survival in the market. 
•	Their competitiveness depends on innovation in achieving lower costs and higher quality effects that will satisfy potential customers, but also on their ability to create new products (e.g. improving products and services, individual approach to the client, taking care of the environment, etc.)
•	Private sector perspective: investing in capacities and facilities that will ensure profitability and minimize investment risks. 
•	Public sector perspective: address infrastructure and investments that are often not profitable
Entrepreneurship in Tourism & Hospitality 
•	Pros  Independence  Market adaptability  The possibility of achieving financial success  Job Security  Family employment
•	Cons  Surge in accountability  Possibility of failure  Susceptibility to fluctuations in the market  Dependence on competition  Financial weakness  Lack of knowledge and expertise  Undeveloped legal regulations
