11th unit
Social entrepreneurship is the organization of a business around specific social and/or environmental causes.
Not-for-profit initiatives in search of alternative funding strategies, or management schemes to create social value. A means to alleviate social problems and catalyze social transformation
The social element
•	Altruism 
•	Ethical motives, moral responsibility, Combine resources to address a social problem and thereby alter existing social structures, Catalyzes social transformation by meeting social needs, Value creation is focused on the social value while economic value creating is seen as a necessary condition to ensure financial viability 
The entreprenual element
•	For profit VS Not for profit, Nature of the social needs addressed, Amount of resource needed, Scope of raising capital, Ability to capture economic value, Priority given to social wealth creation, Difficult to capture (economic) value
Types:
•	COMMUNITY SOCIAL ENTRERENEUR - A local isolated social problem, Seeks to serve the social needs of a community within a small geographical area
•	NON-PROFIT SOCIAL ENTRERENEUR -Prioritize social well-being over traditional business needs, Choose to use their power for social good, Usually more business-savvy entrepreneurs who want to use their skills for creating change, While the results often take longer to manifest, they can take effect on a larger scale
•	TRANSFORMATIONAL SOCIAL ENTRERENEUR - Meet the social needs that governments and other businesses aren't currently meeting, What non-profits evolve to with sufficient time and growth, Larger organizations with rules and regulations , Usually recruit and foster talent in-house
•	GLOBAL SOCIAL ENTRERENEUR - Seek to completely change social systems in order to meet major social needs globally, Often where big companies end up when they realize their social responsibility and begin concentrating on positive change as opposed to just profits, Tied to a particular cause and work with other social entrepreneurs to make it happen.
